{"title":"LaTeX renderer","link":"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Oct/15/latex/","date":1792030048000,"content":"\n    \n        <p><strong>Tool:</strong> <a href=\"https://tools.simonwillison.net/latex\">LaTeX renderer</a></p>\n        <p>Inspired by Paul Ford and Aboard Foundary's new web app <a href=\"https://tinylittleoffice.com/\">Tiny Little Office</a>, which provides a suite of web app content editors (a Word Processor, Outliner, Presentation Builder, Spreadsheet, Calculator, Drawing Tool, Diagram Maker, Animator, Calendar, and Trip Planner).</p>\n<p>Tiny Little Office's signature trick is to store all of the document data in the <code>#fragment</code> section of the URL, so every document created using it becomes a bookmarkable and shareable link.</p>\n<p>It also has <a href=\"https://tinylittleoffice.com/llms.txt\">an llms.txt file</a> telling agents how to construct URLs that will render as documents. Claude <a href=\"https://claude.ai/share/239301ff-edde-420e-b433-b9c16fe40a6c\">used that file</a> to generate <a href=\"https://tinylittleoffice.com/#/v1/deck/zXVVdb9tGEHznr1hED2kBSbZcO4UFtIHTuCgQtylstwH6Uq7Io3gRdcfeHS1T_fjtnV1Ssp038r52d2Z2djabZcmmxizpJ78jpnvrerqxCUv0sapsYaixbkM7HzYxS7XZ4ujWls6u65SlwC7aZL1bUmxsabIZHpxMaCmXlnRfG9rVHm~Vvui2xiWykfDK8Ojfc439b5bN6BdP0YQHE6bkPJWceMXR6A8Xhe9cwqFr7Peptm5NXCUT9Kl8klNM3EeyjnrfBVoFv8Nj2TGbaDgU9ZKuHCe_7clXKFVSkMh5Qs2Nluy14nnhtyeTk4fFSWmKzUn_7e~f5_N5LkcfFvlSg1Y~bDkR8omoXrbkLDZ3tS1q4rYl3xqHnCTvvB~vbX2J2k1C7rI8vjzsHSCaEuK3wcRoSmJXknEFrpVZ9r0CWgVeK5QDBBOB1BlkAgCxmry~dgAz~hePj0i1wT5wMnIY_Ohl0FQ~cTN_Aq~yIo~7ZFpaoL5gcc8iOg6bx5QdaHlB8f3Nxykg_pnDpvQ7Rw8cLCuFdyKTSLEF4uQd5QiTS0gTsftDzSHFKZWBd2A5av3sLLAGzPgNqB94AJlV44uN3LmvEVDQl8C88l2iBX14JyS3DUMTY5pjOS0XG14fKjqDcP_qjNlLTVLLLe_o_fWPN1f319MBSN4a4mbtUXi9lbL3ttW81vgQFvcgcIg0XjwwPv7SDjeBhnBqEpW2kGI49HIu4Bw0fqAcGwRdcdMc6O6fXdAKAcBurA9dWSrYR_AjddGIuH1ayqNbvHft1o2N9RTqc5IIUi~5J4fCAHVEXC5jbZBa1TkNheUjdbF3iR_ndGtaw3i_9RYcr1jwdtAPaLQuJrwhGa2MNGdsTdOAI3DxTEii4hH2b2AivFEd_XZ7M4tcGQH_XZ~gjZVB4oak_9~cd6FZ0htaWQkNmRZQCBdj_1zN_iCe7el0dpkLWZDTf7kWmP~p_Yq_YMBNYQZpd7HjBprLp8OpmViHbRpSDKiCDNWLBo9xxpQatQCyM21DLD8rSNQ6uBwKtgU71eGUVj1Fu0dJeZ4rsnplPL7ioL93CTKkf4aiMyFRhIqFi_PLs~y9qRp0aDmIp8fy4vzsIrsR32y5bzzwxtrl6UKCiFgu5hejLNSKozTg2EdjGz11EioV2kYXpM6VgPZsenp6~gSwaKP2IcF_fLeuxfKgoJjkRBKHi8~43Zh~SZ_qXgyYAMTrpJAKwQDD9ErvJ5Qi7amGdBT1lGpuKl1cKf~DwhDDDTxoj3N6HeFVvfSR9pp1ChBVbJs4oPSqtLA3uAN8zWMjqExfaZshma~~zqVmbWqYGN7j1s4_q3l4MSXG7BunwnFwpCQ~tKTrRwy7KI4l940rdXS8BUK7fLR6HlxI29ylIfH8bZkfJ4HYrSRyGBfztk2PJzIBSFpNOI2jtaLLYbs4~KsWK~MGpgAz10G8pHyYTRP0_Xc5EOm~0CmE89L2ihrnoVUxkfRi8sJzHYTonVE_Ua0cR3Shm1H9CXPLNP2XjovGNI3M0_1smG4C4P8\">this link to a deck</a> that explains how the URL format works:</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Screenshot of a slide deck editor in a web app with a dark-themed presentation open. The document title field reads &quot;How a Tiny Little Office link works&quot;, with a progress ring, share icon and menu icon at top right. Below is a formatting toolbar with an &quot;Insert&quot; dropdown followed by icon buttons for play, bold, italic, underline, strikethrough, highlight, code, link, superscript, emoji, outdent, indent, layout and color palette. The main canvas shows slide 1 with a blue chain-link icon and the text: &quot;The whole document is the link&quot; &quot;No server, no database, no account&quot; &quot;Everything after the # stays in your browser&quot; and a slide counter &quot;1 / 9&quot;. Below the canvas is a &quot;SPEAKER NOTES&quot; heading. A sidebar on the right shows slide thumbnails. Slide 1 (selected): &quot;The whole document is the link&quot; &quot;No server, no database, no account&quot; &quot;Everything after the # stays in your browser&quot;. Slide 2: &quot;Anatomy of a link&quot; with bullets &quot;tinylittleoffice.com/#/v1/deck/y7Vj...&quot; &quot;v1: the format version&quot; &quot;deck: which app opens it&quot; &quot;y: the mode letter&quot; &quot;7Vj...: the document, compressed and encoded&quot;. Slide 3: &quot;Step 1: write it as text&quot; with bullets &quot;Every document is TLO, a Markdown variant&quot; &quot;Slides split on --- lines&quot; &quot;Charts, drawings and animations are fenced blocks&quot; &quot;This deck is about 1 KB of plain text&quot;. Slide 4: &quot;Step 2: squeeze it&quot; with bullets &quot;Raw DEFLATE, the same algorithm as zip and gzip&quot; &quot;z: plain DEFLATE&quot; &quot;y: DEFLATE with a preset dictionary&quot; &quot;r: no compression at all&quot;.\" src=\"/media/1c6b6bdbec09a51431f5b1bb68a24d28be49752387f27705691f1c2aabd78fac.webp\" /></p>\n<p>Tiny Little Office inspired me <a href=\"https://claude.ai/code/session_017KJrPqqWVHLqr4E7TLPnpt\">to have Claude Code</a> (Opus 5.5 Extra) build a similar tool that works with LaTeX. Here's the sequence of prompts I used:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>I want a tool where you can link to it with a fragment hash #code-here and it treats that code as LaTeX and renders it really effectively. It should also support a prefix which means the latex is compressed with a custom compression dictionary optimized for latex. Use the best available latex render library from a CDN (consider WebAssembly too). If loaded without a fragment hash show an editor you can paste latex into and then toggle to a \"rendered\" view. That rendered view should be bookmarkable. The #fragment view should let you switch to edit source too.</code></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Since it was working in <a href=\"https://github.com/simonw/tools\">my tools repo</a> I told it to take inspiration from the <code>&lt;iframe sandbox=\"...\"&gt;</code> mechanism used by my existing <a href=\"https://tools.simonwillison.net/markdown-svg-renderer\">SVG in Markdown tool</a> as protection against XSS risks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>Safe from XSS? We could run in an iframe sandbox like we do on the SVG markdown render project</code></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I had it copy the Gist/URL loading feature from that tool as well:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>We should have an option to load latex from a URL (or gist) similar to the markdown SVG tool</code></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Here's <a href=\"https://tools.simonwillison.net/latex#~1jZq9DoIwEIB3nuKGbigJJS5svoAOMJuQ4EBEJWJjGHh3765A_8DIQtJfIMdR-n0uCV9l4DOs_p8ua3ZZKK2anOsaTqya9AZnHhMo3qqewKWPMh2SOQOFkhSJtmruY1QungBJFk-cQLsspAF8qgFPWKQ6FmdYdCB7oseX5HVNfgE-HGmM2OPATDfAlCssyLcLGI4BONwZ7E8pby5KWoilFBK0n5T4lMeHgQaMMsYjS6hrG61G4Micmcj2EN48tyVgHpzNKMBEHuxEbV0dZhsJwZB7_Lux0mR4YDc9HTd1bnGaki0V1nr6kGHSI8NAwCWaVjeAPyYUNAYxUZt8k2aKlODPRQq7JIYMS-VKaQwHrMmoxqWeLlT6Ag\">an example link</a>. The tool uses <a href=\"https://github.com/nodeca/pako\">Pako 2.1.0</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/mathjax/MathJax\">MathJax 4.1.3</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/arena-ai/faster-latex\">faster-latex 0.3.1</a>, and <a href=\"https://github.com/cure53/DOMPurify\">DOMPurify 3.4.16</a>.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Screenshot of a web app titled &quot;LaTeX renderer&quot; with a split-pane layout. The top bar has an Edit/Rendered toggle, a blue Copy link button, and a Share button. The left pane, labeled LaTeX source with a &quot;Document · faster-latex (WASM)&quot; badge, has a URL/GitHub Gist input with a Load button, a code editor containing a LaTeX document, and rows of example buttons (Quadratic formula, Gaussian integral, Maxwell, Attention, Matrix, Chemistry, Prose, TikZ, Document). The right pane shows the typeset output: a paper titled &quot;Sums of Odd Numbers&quot; by A. Student, with a section stating a theorem that the first n odd numbers sum to n squared, a Proof section with numbered equations, and a bulleted list of small cases.\" src=\"/media/fa37785b8e2e535e533bd2b66be6817fc25a07a913577f3686fabbf4f6b37cb6.webp\" /></p>\n    \n    \n        <p>Tags: <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/latex\">latex</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/paul-ford\">paul-ford</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/tools\">tools</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/urls\">urls</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/ai\">ai</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/generative-ai\">generative-ai</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/llms\">llms</a></p>\n    \n\n","author":"Simon Willison","siteTitle":"Simon Willison’s Weblog","siteHash":"1b599216bd27a00f15d67a3ed9719c64148630c70244b6dd8e743f17cb45527e","entryHash":"de7de85ffee3a161ec77f20fb5302e42af0f2c44fc27721b8ffdd042aa08fa3c","category":"Tech"}